## The checker and the one library

- F-64 **The compiled checker refuses every type that an api declares, trait or object, that extends a trait whose `comprises` clause has `...`, whether the clause lists it or not (row 354, open).** So the form `comprises { T, ... }` cannot stand in an api that declares `T`. The refusal runs only when the checker checks an api, so a component's types may extend such a trait. The specification's text allows `...` in an api's clause, and an extender that the clause lists (`Specification/basic/traits.tex`, "Trait Declarations"). Only a draft note refuses the api's own declaration of an extender, and a release build leaves draft notes out. The team's `compiler_tests/XXX3q` and `XXX10p` pin the refusal, so a fix that skips it for a listed type turns them red. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-library-defects/REPORT.md`, "Defects and their homes".
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-64"); "trait" widened to every declared type, objects included, as the refusal runs (TypeHierarchyChecker.scala:273-278, :338-341; Compiled3.q.fsi:13-14; row 354); "the rendered specification" replaced by the text against a draft note, since the tree builds in draft mode, which renders notes (Specification/fortress/fortress.tex:33-45; traits.tex:161-162, :247-254, :277-283); the api-only scope from isApi (TypeHierarchyChecker.scala:48, :338)
- F-65 **The checker memoizes `TypeAnalyzer.parents` and `excludesClause` per trait table, and the memo changes no answer.** `FORTRESS_ANALYZER_CLAUSES_CACHE=false` turns it off. By reading, both functions depend only on the table and the type. One differential on climb batch 7C's tree gave the same errors at the same sites with the memo off and on: 626 at the distance stage and 75 at the count stage. With the memo off, the count stage's 900-second limit stopped it; a copy with the limit raised took 1,149 s, against 112 s with the memo on. Today the stage takes about 63 s with the memo on (F-240); its time with the memo off has not been measured since. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-analyzer-memo/REPORT.md`, "Why the memo changes no answer"; `reviews/row-488-probe.md`, "The answer".
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-65"); the timing bounded to its one measurement on climb batch 7C's tree (reviews/row-488-probe.md, "The answer", item 3, and "What was run": run 2 stopped by the limit, run 5 a copy with the limit raised), and "so turning it off is no control for anything" dropped, since only that measurement supports it; moved here: "so it does not cause row 488's moves", row 488 being a duplicate of row 737, fixed (the checker's errors in big operators' bodies changed with what it had queried earlier in the same run)
- F-66 **The checker-count stage's table, `gate/checker-count.txt`, lands with every batch beside the gate's `summary.txt`.** Its total at the last landed gate is F-53's count. Step 8 of a batch's gate compares it with the newest landed table. The total is never red on its own, as P-39 rules, since a fix can raise it and the effects of two rungs on it do not add up. The gate is red on a crash line that no rung declared in its manifest (`expectedCheckerCrash`), on a table with no total, and on a stale shadow: the instrumented copy of `StaticChecker` beside the stage (`checker-count/shadow-src/`), when the tracked `StaticChecker` no longer matches the checksum that `run.sh` records. Detail: `coordinator/climb-batch-workflow.md`, "The checker count, and a rung whose test is that stage".
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-66"); the shadow said from climb-batch-workflow.md:226, climb-batch-workflow.js:1129-1130 and checker-count/run.sh:22-33, :96-98; the declared crash from P-39; step 8 is the batch's gate's (climb-batch-workflow.md:220); P-39 named as the owner of the never-red rule
- F-67 **The compiled type checker checks `nat` and `int` static parameters.** A size is a size parameter's name, a literal or an operation on sizes. Two sizes are equal when their written forms are, once each operation on numerals is computed, so `2 3` equals `6` but `m n` does not equal `n m` (F-146). The checker refuses to infer a `bool`, `dim` or `unit` static argument (`XXXNatBoolChecker`). It does not compare `bool` arguments, so `object F extends { Flag[\true\], Flag[\false\] }`, two instantiations of one trait, passes it, and code generation then stops on it (row 406, open, a part of P-40 still to build). A call that leaves a size unknown is refused, "Could not infer static argument ... without context"; F-69 gives when an overload set is refused and when it is not. It builds P-40. Gated by `compiler_tests/NatInferredChecker`, `NatWrittenChecker`, `NatMethodChecker`, `NatExportChecker` and `XXXNatMismatchChecker`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-nat-checker/REPORT.md` section 1.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-67"); "refuses a bool, dim or unit parameter by name" corrected to the refusal of inference (STypesUtil.scala:585-606; XXXNatBoolChecker.fss; rung-nat-checker/REPORT.md section 1); row 406 said as its own defect, the uncompared bool arguments, which P-40 lists as still to build; folding from rung-size-expressions/REPORT.md section 1 (Formula.foldSize, nEq); the tests from rung-nat-checker/REPORT.md section 6; the overload-set case left to F-69, which gives its limits (rows 400, 446)
- F-69 **The compiled checker refuses a call whose most specific arm failed only on a size that the call cannot fix, when that arm's parameter types are free of the size.** The message is "Could not infer static argument nat n without context". It drops instead an arm whose unknown size is in its own parameter types (row 400, open). It lets through a call whose argument's static type lies above the arm's domain, and the compiled run dies reading the size (row 446, open). It builds P-40. Gated by `compiler_tests/NatKnownSizeArm`, which compiles and runs a call that fixes the size, and `XXXNatUnknownSize*`, which pin the refusal's message; rows 400 and 446 have no test. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-unknown-size-arm/REPORT.md` section 10.
  by: the clarity pass; note: the body's condition put into the title, so that the title claims no more than the body; the rows' state and each test's case said (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-69"; Functionals.scala:255-265, :765-772)
- F-70 **The compiled checker checks a static argument written at a dotted method call against its parameter's bound, but not one written at a top-level function call.** At a dotted call it puts the written arguments into each bound and refuses a call whose argument misses one, "No such method" (row 651, fixed). At a top-level call it matches kinds only, so `n[\String\]("d")` passes for `n[\T extends Number\](s: String): T` (row 470, open). Gated by `compiler_tests/MethodStaticArgsBoundNamesOther*`, which accept a dotted call whose written arguments meet their bounds. No test pins the refusal of an argument that misses its bound; a probe showed it (`rung-checker-contexts/REPORT.md` section 6). Row 470 can have no gated test, since the harness has no form for a program that the specification refuses and the checker accepts. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-checker-contexts/REPORT.md` section 1.
  by: the clarity pass; note: split (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-70"); F-70 keeps the checker's check of a written static argument by call form, which F-87 points to; the library's bounds and walk's stop on a written Object (row 471) moved to a new entry; row 470's example and its want of a gated test from the gap ledger; the tests' scope from MethodStaticArgsBoundNamesOther.fss and MethodStaticArgsBoundNamesOtherSameName.fss, which assert 1 and 11
- F-?1 **No static parameter of the interpreter's library is bounded by `Object`, so no library declaration meets walk's stop on that bound: under walk, an argument whose type is a tuple, `()` or an arrow, at a parameter whose type is a type variable bounded by `Object`, stops the program with an `InterpreterBug`, not a type error (row 471, open).** The stop needs a bound written `Object`, since walk installs a written bound before it unifies the arguments (row 471). The library's result-only parameters are unbounded, as P-48 decides (F-84). Six functions of `FortressLibrary` write the bound `extends Any`, which admits tuples, `()` and arrows: `cast`, `instanceOf`, `identity`, `shared`, `localize` and `copy` (`Library/FortressLibrary.fss:33-72`; its api declares the first five so, and holds `copy` in a comment). The specification's examples and the compiler tests write `extends Object` (`SpecData/examples/basic/Fun.Decl.fss:19`, `ProjectFortress/other_compiler_tests/Go1a.fss:15`). Row 471 has no test. Evidence: `git grep -nE '\[\\[^\\]*extends +Object' -- Library ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin` prints nothing; walk's stop is from the probe that row 471 records, no longer in the tree. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-result-bounds/REPORT.md` section 10.
  by: the clarity pass; note: split from F-70 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-70"), which held these claims beside the checker's check by call form; "six, cast among them" named as FortressLibrary's six functions (FortressLibrary.fss:33, :39, :49, :65, :69, :72; FortressLibrary.fsi:51-53 holds copy in a comment), of which only cast's parameter is result-only; walk's stop said from row 471; the one `where {T extends Object}` is in Library/incomplete/, off the source path
- F-71 **The compiled checker does not narrow the variable of the shorthand form `typecase x of T => ...`, neither path accepts the binding form `typecase x = e of`, and the clause-binding form `typecase e of y: T => ... end` runs on both paths.** So write the clause-binding form, as the library does (F-134): its `cast` writes `typecase x of x':T => x' ... end`. In the shorthand form, the checker refuses a clause that uses `x` as a `T`, and such a clause runs under walk only; a clause that does not, as in the library's `instanceOf`, passes. The binding form parses as the equality `x = e`. Only the specification's grammar has it, under the team's note that the section awaits the pattern-matching proposal (`typecase.tex:15`). Row 460 records both refusals, and no test gates them. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-exclusion-remainder/REPORT.md` section 15.
  by: the clarity pass; note: the three forms shown, and the missing test said (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-71"; row 460; typecase.tex:15, :126-133); "the shorthand form runs under walk only" narrowed to a clause that uses the variable at the narrowed type, since a clause that does not passes the checker (FortressLibrary.fss:33-43; the team's Compiled5.o in compiler_tests/AfterTypeChecking.test)
- F-72 **The compiled checker reads a `comprises` clause for a generic subtrait as the team's 2012 texts do (Luchangco's Types chapter and Welterweight; P-44): the subtrait may extend the closed trait when the trait table knows at least one type that immediately extends the subtrait, and each such type is below a listed type.** It builds P-44. This rule leaves the ellipsis refusal of F-64 (row 354, open) as it was. Open: an extender in another compilation unit escapes the check, the hole that P-44 leaves, on phase 3's road with no batch named (row 487). Parked for the curator: the verdict changes when the extender renames its type variable (row 489), and the extenders are found by the simple text of the trait's name (row 490). Gated by `compiler_tests/ComprisesGenericSubtrait`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-comprises-checker/REPORT.md` section 6.
  by: the clarity pass; note: row 487 placed on phase 3's road, not parked (PLAN, "Rows on phase 3's road with no batch named"); rows 489 and 490 parked (PLAN, "Off the path, parked"; review-queue item 13); each row said by what it does; "the ellipsis refusal stays" said as this change's scope, from the history's "the ellipsis refusal (row 354) is untouched"; the 2012 texts named from P-44 (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-72")
- F-73 **The compiled checker infers a generic's static arguments with coercion: a type parameter that is the whole type of each parameter it occurs in, as `T` in `pick[\T\](x: T, y: T)`, takes the narrowest type that every one of its arguments converts into and its bound permits.** It looks for that type among its arguments' types, their coercion targets and its bound, the bound only when it is a trait type other than `Object` or `Any`. It builds P-74 and P-49. A coerced declaration ranks below one that needs no coercion. A tie among coerced declarations is refused, "Ambiguous coercion", outside three narrow cases (the report's section 3.2). When the tied declarations differ only at positions where the call passes a numeral, the checker reads the numeral as `ZZ32`, or as `ZZ64` or `ZZ` when its value does not fit; that reading is a default that waits for the curator's review (review-queue item 209). The coercion attempt refuses a call beyond 64 combinations of candidate types (row 506) and a call with an untyped function argument (row 507). Gated by `compiler_tests/NatLitArgChecker`, `CoercionGenericFnCompiledRungC` and `InferCoercionShapes`, which F-51 also cites, and the refusal of a tie by `XXXInferAmbiguousCoercion`. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-inference-checker/REPORT.md` section 3.2.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten for a reader without the batch (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-73"); "a lone type parameter" said as the code chooses it (Functionals.scala:341-342; report section 3.2), with InferCoercionShapes' pick as its example; the bound's condition from Functionals.scala:348-356; the three cases that keep a tie from report section 3.2, "The ambiguity check"; the numeral reading marked as the default of review-queue item 209, not a rule of P-74 or P-49; rows 506 and 507 said by their shapes; the inference's tests named (the history's former text; F-51)
- F-74 **Under walk, an integer numeral takes its type from its value: a `ZZ32` if it fits 32 bits, a `ZZ64` if it fits 64 bits, a `ZZ` above that; it stays so until walk has the checker's static types, since without them a list of numerals and a generic's static argument take their types from the values.** Its run-time type is the library's value object `Int`, `Long` or `BigNum`, below `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` and `ZZ` (`ProjectFortress/LibraryBuiltin/FortressBuiltin.fsi:125`, `:128`, `:223`). Walk instantiates a generic at it, so a `Box[\Int\]` is a type apart from `Box[\ZZ32\]` and dispatches past it (row 364). F-74 is why P-76's walk half waits; F-79 is the library half, built. Probe Q (`compile-ladder/plan-n/probe-q/PROBE-Q.md`) measured walk with every integer numeral an `IntLiteral`, the numeral's own type in the library. It ran before climb batch N's second run and before the library half landed. Alone, the switch changed 59 test verdicts. With four devices (a numeral at an `AnyIntegral` parameter read as `ZZ32`, `ZZ64` or `ZZ`, a tuple of numerals converted at dispatch, `asif` converting, a varargs list converted at dispatch), the library loaded and both microGPT checks kept their values, but 24 passing interpreter tests failed: 6 by design, 3 of them team tests that assert a `ZZ32`; 9 for want of static types (rows 364, 510); and 9 walk gaps. A size used as a value is a `ZZ32` up to 2^31-1 and a `ZZ64` above (row 486; F-140). Detail: `reviews/numeral-switch-judgement.md` sections 2 and 3.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-74"); "an Int" corrected to the type by width, as P-76 states and FIntLiteral.make builds (FIntLiteral.java:40-56); Int, Long and BigNum said as the library's value objects that walk instantiates at (FortressBuiltin.fsi:125, :128, :223; row 364; numeral-switch-judgement.md section 2); the 24 failures bounded to probe Q, its four devices (judgement section 2, its second item, and the paragraph on device A) and its tree, beside the 59 verdicts that the switch alone changed; a size above 2^31-1 from F-140
- F-75 **The compiled checker checks the Return Type Rule over every instance, and a positional rule: when two generic declarations in the more-specific relation declare as many static parameters of their own, those parameters agree in kind by position, and the more specific return type is a subtype of the other once the other's parameters are read as its own by position.** The equal-count scope and the comparison of kinds are the rung's choices; review-queue item 60 lists the scope for the curator's review. It builds P-45. Checking every instance, as P-45 asks, it also refuses a generic declaration beside a plain one whose domain is an object type, a pair that the type group's 2011 paper (`Papers/Types/`) accepts, since the paper sets aside the instances whose domains cannot meet (row 542, open). Review-queue item 57 lists the difference for the curator's review; no work needs it. In the interpreter's library the two rules refuse no pair: the distance holds no return-type or overloading error (F-53). Gated by `compiler_tests/OverloadGeneric*` and `OverloadBoundNamesForced`, which accept; `XXXOverloadReturnEveryInstance` and `XXXOverloadPermutedStaticParams`, which pin the two rules' refusals; and `XXXOverloadReturnObjectDomain`, which pins row 542's. Detail: `compile-ladder/rung-return-type-rule/decision-record.md` sections 1 and 2.
  by: the clarity pass; note: rewritten (consolidation-2026-10-22/evidence/facts/f5/proposals.md, "F-75"); the handle "answer 9" dropped; the condition of equal counts of own static parameters, and the kinds and return type compared, from satisfiesPositionalRule (OverloadingOracle.scala:150-176); the scope and the kinds given as the rung's choices (decision-record.md section 2, "Its scope", "Its kinds"; review-queue item 60); the object-domain refusal said as following from P-45's every-instance construction (review-queue item 57, "Landed", "Needed by: never"); the refusals' tests named
